  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1258 PM MDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nIt's a hot one today with temperatures early this afternoon already\nin mid to upper 90s along the I-25 Corridor, and approaching \nrecords in some spots (Denver's record is 99F set in 2023). There\nwas some slight relief over the northeast plains due to outflow \nfrom earlier Nebraska convection, so temperatures have been \nrunning a few degrees below expectations there. Given the strong \nsurface heating and steep low/mid level lapse rates along the\nFront Range, we are seeing high based convection develop. These \nwill be capable of producing strong microburst winds as DCAPE has \ngrown into the 1400-1600 J/kg range. Meanwhile, farther east the \nlow level moisture is holding steady with dewpoints in the lower \nto mid 60s in the easterly flow. While this easterly surge is \nexpected to weaken during peak heating, it will provide for higher\ninstability and a richer storm environment should storms be able \nto fire. Thus, a couple severe storms will be possible through \nthis evening over the northeast plains given MLCAPE of 1200-2000 \nJ/kg and bulk shear near 30 kts.\n\nFor Saturday, monsoonal moisture will increase as the upper level\nridge axis tilts ever so slowly eastward. Precipitable water\nvalues are forecast to reach near 150% of normal. However, the\nairmass is quite warm so we think most storms that originate over\nthe mountains and then push onto the I-25 Corridor in the\nafternoon will still be relatively high based, and thus mostly \noutflow dominated. We think the majority of the storms with more \nwidespread and beneficial rains will develop just east of the I-25\nCorridor where outflows intersect with a richer low level \nenvironment. That's where HREF ensembles show higher concentration\nof stronger storms. Locally heavy rain totals upwards of 1.5-2 \ninches will be possible there, and potentially the Palmer Divide, \nbased on the environment and HREF Local Probability Matched Means \n(LPMM). Meanwhile, there will also be a threat of a few severe \nstorms as MLCAPE once again grows to 1200-2000 J/kg and bulk shear\nof 25-35 kts is forecast. \n\nFor Sunday, there is good agreement that the mid and upper levels\nwill dry slightly as a more westerly component develops under a\nflattening ridge. Thus, storm coverage should decrease a little,\nin the absence of any forcing mechanisms. A few severe storms \nwill once again be possible across the plains as we expect \nsufficient low level moisture and instability, with the greatest \nrisk once again hovering just east of the I-25 Corridor. \n\nFor much of next week, deterministic forecasts and ensembles are \naveraging toward flat ridging and weak westerly flow aloft. That \nlikely keeps us too zonal for deep monsoonal moisture, but just \nenough for scattered storms most afternoons and evening. While \nindividual model runs vary, there is still potential for a weak \nbackdoor cool front or two. They will offer up very little in the \nway of cooling, but should keep us a few degrees \"cooler\" (mostly\nlower 90s for highs on the plains and I-25 Corridor) versus the \nfull on summer heat experienced today. Ensembles are supportive of\na decrease in precipitable water (PW) by the end of next week, \nwhich would support less, or only isolated, storm possibilities \nand a return of much above normal temperatures. &&",
